{"author":"RebarBotNava","author_email":"joeoakhart+bot@navapbc.com","author_id":"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d","author_sig":"{\"payload\": \"\", \"payloadType\": \"application/vnd.rebar.authorship.v1+json\", \"signatures\": [{\"keyid\": \"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d\", \"sig\": \"\"}]}","data":{"alias":"skipping-unamorous-massasauga","creation_channel":"cli","description":"## Symptom\n\n`tests/e2e/test_editor_e2e.py::test_emitted_bpmn_has_no_illegal_ids` intermittently fails in CI as\n`worker 'gw0' crashed` / `[gw0] node down: Not properly terminated`, with **no traceback and no\nassertion** — the signature of a killed xdist worker rather than a test failure. It reproduces on\nunrelated patchsets: observed on Gerrit changes 2592, 2593 and 2594 within a ~30 minute window on\n2026-09-03, and absent on 2596 seven minutes later. Two independent investigations converged on the\nsame mechanism.\n\n## Root cause\n\n`tests/e2e/conftest.py::bpmn_harness` is a **session-scoped fixture that provisions the Node\ntoolchain lazily, inside the first e2e test's setup** — `npm install` (`conftest.py:51`) then\n`npm run build`/esbuild (`conftest.py:57`). Because the fixture is requested first by\n`test_emitted_bpmn_has_no_illegal_ids`, the entire cold install is billed to that one test.\n\nThat cost is charged against `timeout = 300` with `timeout_method = \"thread\"`\n(`pyproject.toml:719-720`), which deliberately carries **no `timeout_func_only`**, so the budget\ncovers fixture setup. `.github/workflows/_build-and-test.yml:773-775` documents the consequence in\nthe tree already: the thread method calls `os._exit(1)`, so an over-tight budget \"kills the whole\nxdist worker (`node down: Not properly terminated`)\" instead of reporting `Failed: Timeout`.\n\n**Measured, not inferred.** In *passing* CI run 33811108275 the setup is the slowest single span in\nthe suite: 101.26s (py3.11), 120.93s (py3.12), 89.69s (py3.13). Cold in a fresh worktree it measured\n98.37s. So the normal case sits at 90-120s of a 300s budget — a 2.5-3.3x margin that npm-registry\nand runner variance can consume. Timing on the failing legs corroborates a clean 300s expiry:\npy3.12 ran 386s from pytest start to `node down` (300s + ~86s startup); py3.13, which additionally\ncarries `--cov`, ran 486s (300s + ~186s startup).\n\n**Aggravators:**\n- There is **no `setup-node` step and no npm cache** anywhere in `.github/workflows/`, so every CI\n  run pays a cold registry install.\n- The two provisioning `subprocess.run` calls carry **no `timeout=`**, unlike every other subprocess\n  in the same file (`:74` `timeout=60`, `:118` `timeout=150`). Their only bound is the global\n  pytest-timeout, whose kill method takes the worker down instead of producing a diagnosable failure.\n- Under `-n 4 --dist worksteal` **each worker runs the session-scoped fixture independently**, so up\n  to four workers can race `npm install`/`npm run build` into the *same* `tests/e2e/js/node_modules`\n  and `dist/`. This did not fire in the observed runs (only one worker paid the cost) but is\n  unguarded.\n\nOnly one test fails because gw0 dies paying the cold install; xdist replaces it, and the replacement\nfinds `node_modules`/`dist` already populated, so the remaining three e2e tests pass.\n\n## Why this is a bug and not a retry\n\nPer AGENTS.md, a flaky test is root-caused, never retried. The class of defect is that **toolchain\nprovisioning is charged to a test's timeout budget**, and that the failure mode is a worker kill\nrather than a diagnosable error. Both are fixable in-tree.\n\n## Acceptance criteria\n\n1. The provisioning subprocesses at `tests/e2e/conftest.py:51` and `:57` carry explicit `timeout=`\n   values, and exceeding one produces a clear failure or skip that **names the provisioning step** —\n   never a worker kill and never a bare `node down`.\n2. Toolchain provisioning does not consume a test's timeout budget. **This must be portable**: the\n   fix cannot be CI-only (see the blocking `project.portability` criterion) — the in-fixture path\n   must keep working in a checkout with no CI provider. A portable target (e.g. `make e2e-deps`\n   running `npm ci` + `npm run build`) invoked as a pre-pytest step, with the in-fixture provisioning\n   retained as the fallback, satisfies this.\n3. CI caches the npm store keyed on `tests/e2e/js/package-lock.json`, and uses `npm ci` (a lockfile\n   exists) rather than `npm install`.\n4. Concurrent provisioning by two or more xdist workers is made safe — a file lock around\n   install+build, or provisioning moved out of the fixture entirely.\n5. **Verified in real time, not by waiting for a recurrence.** From a cold state (`node_modules` and\n   `dist` removed) run the e2e suite under the CI xdist configuration and observe: the run completes\n   with no worker kill, and provisioning time is not attributed to any single test's timeout budget.\n   Additionally, artificially stall provisioning (stub `npm` to sleep past the new explicit\n   `timeout=`) and assert the result is the named, diagnosable failure from AC1 rather than\n   `node down`. Both are deterministic and complete in one run.\n\n## File impact\n\n`tests/e2e/conftest.py`, `.github/workflows/_build-and-test.yml`, `Makefile`.","id":"9a17-e0b3-7aa6-4091","parent_id":"","priority":2,"tags":["bug","tests","ci","flake"],"ticket_type":"bug","title":"e2e Node toolchain is provisioned inside a test's timeout budget, killing an xdist worker"},"env_id":"b8a354e0-12c3-4cf2-bbf1-fcc9685d5126","event_type":"CREATE","timestamp":1788479648120928001,"uuid":"b09b5baa-2d82-46bf-bd79-e9821f633335"}